      ":question/problem": "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$"
